{
  "case_id": "2004-04-637",
  "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE O'CONNOR: Counsel, I -- I think he was given the death sentence. Is that right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your Honor, the State said that they -- they thought there was evidence to support that. They said, I don't believe it's necessary for this court to conclude he was the actual shooter, and that's in the joint appendix at 186. So the death penalty is appropriate --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Why do you argue that if there is no due process violation with respect to the actual plea, there is, therefore, no due process violation with respect to the sentence?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "He said there's ample evidence from which the court could conclude --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Why -- why is that? I mean, it seems to me due process requires that there be enough evidence to -- for a jury to find beyond a reasonable doubt that -- that the person was the shooter."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the State has made two arguments: one that Stumpf was the triggerman, one that Wesley was the triggerman."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice STEVENS",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, and the original sentencer might -- might not have done the same thing. We don't really know that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice BREYER",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There's no doubt that they could have -- the trier of fact could have reached the same conclusion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. COLE",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0001-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the intermediate appellate court expressly said that its determination did not turn on who had pulled the trigger, that they would have reached the same conclusion independent of who pulled the trigger."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice SCALIA",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Could -- could I get one fact? Wesley didn't confess or we don't know that he confessed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What should -- what -- was Wesley then denied due process? Because at the time of Stumpf's initial plea hearing, Eastman hadn't appeared and there was only Stumpf's testimony that he was not the triggerman and the prosecutor's evidence that he was."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Freedman",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FREEDMAN: We -- we proffer to the Court that they shouldn't be taking inconsistent positions as to -- in a death penalty case in the sentencing --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE O'CONNOR: Well, do you concede that the evidence presented in the Stumpf proceeding was sufficient to support a finding of guilt as an aider and abetter? Was there enough evidence that the fact finder could so find?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: What is your best case, Mr. Freedman, for the idea that an inconsistency, such as you say was present here, is a violation of due process?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: I'm -- I'm not getting it. What do you mean?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Can I ask you a procedural question here? When I read the -- the Ohio Supreme Court opinion, my impression of their due process part of it is that they found a violation because of the inconsistency between the Wesley trial and the Stumpf sentencing proceeding."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "defense counsel",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0002-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FREEDMAN: They -- they argued generally in -- in the record, and it was -- it was somewhat cryptic. They argued that it was unfair --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cole",
      "case_id": "2004-04-637",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d88c0ed45f88bb1ee08eb27858e93102cb44ce9df59dc8ecaa91ac8e21ba346b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d88c0ed45f88-0002-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COLE: Justice Ginsburg, to respond to your question, under Ohio law you do not need to -- to plan to kill the witness in order to have specific intent for aggravated murder. I would refer the Court to the In re Washington and State v. Scott cases that we cite in our brief that say that participation in an aggravated robbery where that's going to be done through force or violence or in a manner reasonably likely to result in death is sufficient to support a -- an intent -- a -- an inference of specific intent."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
